<h1>And Winter Came... (2008)</h1>

<a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="https://amzn.to/48dzSaQ" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">
    <Image  src={awc} alt="And Winter Came... (2008)"  width="300" height="300" /></a>

<h2>Album Overview</h2>

<p>Irish singer and composer <strong>Enya</strong> released her seventh studio album, <strong><em>And Winter Came...</em></strong>, in November 2008.</p>

<p>Issued internationally by <strong>Warner Bros. Records</strong> on <strong>7 November 2008</strong> and by <strong>Reprise Records</strong> in the United States on <strong>11 November 2008</strong>, the album immerses listeners in a rich seasonal ambiance.</p>

<p>Recorded between 2006 and 2008 at <strong>Aigle Studio</strong> in <strong>Killiney, County Dublin</strong>, the collection features ten original English tracks alongside traditional carols and instrumentals.</p>

<p><a href="https://amzn.to/48dzSaQ" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Stream or download Enya songs</a></p>

<h3>Commercial Success</h3>

<p>The album achieved massive commercial success worldwide, selling over <strong>3,000,000 copies globally</strong>.</p>

<p>It debuted at <strong>#6 on the UK Albums Chart</strong> and <strong>#8 on the US Billboard 200</strong>, while reaching the top 10 in 21 countries within three weeks of release.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>UK Debut:</strong> #6 on 16 November 2008 with 35,812 copies sold in its first week.</li>
<li><strong>US Charts:</strong> #8 on the Billboard 200 with 92,000 first-week copies, certified <strong>Gold</strong> by the RIAA with over 920,000 sales.</li>
<li><strong>Billboard Highlights:</strong> Spent 1 week at #1 on <strong>Top Holiday Albums</strong> and 4 weeks at #1 on <strong>Top New Age Albums</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Certifications:</strong> Earned <strong>Platinum</strong> certifications in Australia, Belgium, Ireland, New Zealand, and Switzerland, alongside <strong>3× Gold</strong> in Germany.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Concept and Production</h2>

<p>Following her 2006 EP <strong><em>Sounds of the Season: The Enya Collection</em></strong>, Enya set out to create a full-length Christmas album combining traditional carols with new material.</p>

<p>Because winter is her favorite season—describing it as "very reflective" and "very calming"—Enya spent significant time developing musical sketches throughout the year.</p>

<p>During the recording process, Enya noted that she "didn't feel it was right to impose a Christmas theme on certain songs," leading to a broader focus on winter landscapes.</p>

<p>Producer <a href="/roma-ryan-nicky/">Nicky Ryan</a> noted that while they began writing for a holiday record, it evolved into "an Enya album based in a winter landscape where Christmas arrives here and there."</p>

<p>Lyricist <a href="/roma-ryan-nicky/">Roma Ryan</a> highlighted the seasonal philosophy, explaining that while some view winter as an ending, she sees it "very much as a new beginning."</p>

<h3>Format and Distribution</h3>

<p>Diverging from previous Enya album cycles that relied on physical CD singles with b-sides, Warner Bros. focused heavily on digital download releases.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Physical Single:</strong> <strong>"Trains and Winter Rains"</strong> served as the album's sole physical CD single release.</li>
<li><strong>Digital Downloads:</strong> Tracks including <strong>"White Is in the Winter Night"</strong>, <strong>"My! My! Time Flies!"</strong>, and <strong>"Dreams Are More Precious"</strong> were issued exclusively as digital singles.</li>
<li><strong>Vinyl Release:</strong> The album received its first <strong>vinyl LP pressings</strong> in the US on 20 October 2017 and in Europe on 12 October 2018.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Track Insights and Composition</h2>

<p>The album showcases a mix of original winter themes, vocal arrangements, and traditional sacred carols.</p>

<h3>Original Highlights</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>"And Winter Came...":</strong> An instrumental track rearranged from <strong>"Midnight Blue"</strong> (the B-side to 2000's "Wild Child"), chosen as the opener to set an autumn-to-winter transition.</li>
<li><strong>"Trains and Winter Rains":</strong> Enya's personal choice for lead single, featuring prominent percussion arrangements and a vocal line incorporating the Christian verse <strong>"Agnus Dei"</strong>. Roma Ryan described it as a dark journey everyone must take when leaving home.</li>
<li><strong>"White Is in the Winter Night":</strong> Conceived as a "21st century Christmas carol," incorporating lyrics that detail traditional holiday colors like mistletoe green, holly red, and candle gold.</li>
<li><strong>"Journey of the Angels":</strong> Written as a slow traditional carol and dedicated to late music video director <strong>Tim Royes</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>"Dreams Are More Precious":</strong> Described by Roma Ryan as a Christmas lullaby, deriving its title directly from her lyrics.</li>
<li><strong>"My! My! Time Flies!":</strong> The final track recorded for the album, written as a tribute to late Irish guitarist <strong>Jimmy Faulkner</strong>. It represents a musical departure for Enya, featuring lead guitar solos by Dubliner <strong>Pat Farrell</strong> and lyrical nods to The Beatles, Elvis, and B.B. King.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Traditional Adaptations</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>"O Come, O Come, Emmanuel":</strong> Sung in Latin from the carol "Veni Veni," inspired by Enya's memories of singing in her boarding school choir.</li>
<li><strong>"Oíche Chiúin (Chorale)":</strong> A re-recording of "Silent Night" in Gaelic after 20 years, utilizing Nicky Ryan's signature "choir of one" technique to layer Enya's vocals.</li>
<li><strong>"Stars and Midnight Blue":</strong> A vocals-added vocal version expanded from the earlier instrumental "Midnight Blue".</li>
<li><strong>"Miraculum":</strong> Released as an exclusive bonus track for the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B001KPYNW0/ref=as_li_tl?ie=UTF8&camp=1789&creative=390957&creativeASIN=B001KPYNW0&linkCode=as2&tag=enylyrallenys-20&linkId=PRFYST3ZTYISYVUS">Amazon.com MP3 download edition</a>.</li>
</ul>

